 [17592186115316 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115316 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115316 #edb/ref 1039 "Using a Normal Approximation to Approximate a Binomial Probability" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115316 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186115145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115316 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e8566d65-0dce-59ca-b019-ddcb02c68e67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115317 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115317 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115317 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115317 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115317 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115317 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115317 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115317 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115317 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115317 #edb/ref 1039 "Using a Normal Approximation to Approximate a Binomial Probability Over an Interval" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115317 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186115146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115317 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "91946709-3ca8-5501-be63-336b06f6c305" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115440 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115440 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115440 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115440 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115440 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115440 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115440 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115440 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115440 #edb/ref 1045 "Normal Approximations of Binomial Distributions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115440 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186116233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115440 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115440 #edb/ref 1050 1080.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115440 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e0acd4c8-93c4-5865-ac51-9faa700a780f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115992 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186114889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115992 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95746" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115992 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115992 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, approximate the probability that a student gets $14$ correct answers on a true/false quiz with $25$ questions if the student answers all questions at random. Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115992 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1c656830-cd06-512a-9850-cdb6ac44625c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115992 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115992 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=25$ and $p=0.5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =25\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =12.5 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =25\\cdot (1-0.5) \\\\  & =12.5 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(25)(0.5)=12.5\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(25)(0.5)(1-0.5)=6.25.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(12.5,6.25).$\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability $P(X=14).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=14)≈P(13.5\\le Y\\le 14.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(13.5\\le Y\\le 14.5) & =P(\\frac{13.5-12.5}{\\sqrt{6.25}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{14.5-12.5}{\\sqrt{6.25}}) \\\\  & ≈P(0.40\\le Z\\le 0.80) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.80)-P(Z<0.40) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.80)-P(Z\\le 0.40) \\\\  & =Φ(0.80)-Φ(0.40) \\\\  & =0.7881-0.6554 \\\\  & =0.1327\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=14)≈0.133.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.1327.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115993 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186114895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115993 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95758" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115993 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115993 #edb/ref 1071 "It is known that $43%$ of the users of a newspaper's website purchase a digital subscription. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, estimate the probability that in a random sample of $200$ users, more than $80$ but less than $105$ of them purchase a digital subscription. Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.09\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.8\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1922\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1894\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1867\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2206\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2177\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2148\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2514\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2483\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2451\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.03\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.04\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,2.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9941\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9943\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9945\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,2.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9956\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9957\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9959\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,2.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9967\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9968\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9969\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115993 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5faab60e-ecea-58c0-a299-eb32601cdfdd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115993 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115993 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=200$ and $p=0.43.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =200\\cdot 0.43 \\\\  & =86 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =200\\cdot (1-0.43) \\\\  & =114 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(200)(0.43)=86\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(200)(0.43)(1-0.43)=49.02.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(86,49.02).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(80<X<105).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(80<X<105)=P(81\\le X\\le 104)≈P(80.5\\le Y\\le 104.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(80.5\\le Y<104.5) & =P(\\frac{80.5-86}{\\sqrt{49.02}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{104.5-86}{\\sqrt{49.02}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.79\\le Z\\le 2.64) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.64)-P(Z<-0.79) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.64)-P(Z\\le -0.79) \\\\  & =Φ(2.64)-Φ(-0.79) \\\\  & =0.9959-0.2148 \\\\  & =0.7811\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(80<X<105)≈0.781.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of\n$0.7798.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116233 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116233 #edb/ref 1061 30951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116233 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f1b69042-4e4f-5d93-b2e6-d2792a279297" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116233 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116234 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116234 #edb/ref 1061 30952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116234 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "029cc3c0-aac1-5ab3-b3dc-fe7cf275acc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116234 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116235 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116235 #edb/ref 1061 30953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116235 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0681c605-1849-50ee-9d50-5be4f585dc4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116235 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116236 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116236 #edb/ref 1061 30954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116236 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a44f609d-29bb-5f64-b769-1c94ec6780fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116236 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116266 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116266 #edb/ref 1061 34580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116266 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "41a4f348-1e37-5763-9563-f9ba63ffa00a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116266 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116267 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116267 #edb/ref 1061 34581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116267 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "88da9ae9-0589-5f70-ab03-1c92544b026c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116267 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116274 #edb/ref 1061 39428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116274 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b9f0c982-62ce-5f5d-9d4d-30e10da26092" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116274 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116536 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose the random variable $X\\sim B(n,p)$ follows a binomial distribution.\n\nThere are certain situations where working with binomial random variables becomes difficult. For example, when the parameter $n$ is large, computing exact probabilities can become problematic due to the large numbers involved.\n\nUnder certain conditions, we can approximate the *discrete* random variable $X$ using a *continuous normal* random variable $Y,$ given by\n\n$$\nY \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mu$ and $\\sigma^2$ are calculated using the formulas for the mean and variance of the binomial random variable $X\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mu = np, \\qquad \\sigma^2 = np(1-p)\n$$\n\nTo determine whether a given binomial distribution can be approximated using a normal distribution, we use the following rule of thumb:\n\n- $np > 5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5.$\n\nTo get a feel for how this works, let's consider a few examples of binomial distributions and their corresponding normal approximations:\n\n- First, consider the case where $n=5$ and $p=0.2.$ In this case, it's straightforward to show that \n\n$$\n\\mu = {\\color{blue}{1}},\\qquad \\sigma^2 = {\\color{red}{0.8}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nnp = 1 < 5,\\qquad n(1-p) = 4 < 5.\n$$\n\nSince $np < 5,$ the normal distribution $N({\\color{blue}{1}},{\\color{red}{0.8}})$ is *not* a good approximation to $B(5, 0.2).$ \n\nThe diagram below compares the two distributions. The yellow rectangles correspond to the values of the PMF of $B(5, 0.2),$ and the smooth blue curve is the PDF of the corresponding normal distribution.\n\n![](images/6a/6a67b3ad65bfedcd25b787ff15e72c575da1215ae47502ff11f97d40de407560.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the case where $n=5$ and $p=0.5.$ In this case, it's straightforward to show that \n\n$$\n\\mu = {\\color{blue}{2.5}},\\qquad \\sigma^2 = {\\color{red}{1.25}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nnp = n(1-p) = 2.5 < 5.\n$$\n\nSince $np = n(1-p) < 5,$ the normal distribution $N({\\color{blue}{2.5}},{\\color{red}{1.25}})$ is *not* a good approximation to $B(5, 0.5).$ \n\n![](images/0a/0a8f2d9ccd78ee885ea3bb8f2f00705f69c520f8ec0588411865399b6ecef046.png)\n\n- Finally, we consider the case where $n=20$ and $p=0.5.$ In this case, it's straightforward to show that \n\n$$\n\\mu = {\\color{blue}{10}},\\qquad \\sigma^2 = {\\color{red}{5}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nnp = n(1-p) = 10 > 5.\n$$\n\nSince $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5,$ the normal distribution $N({\\color{blue}{10}},{\\color{red}{5}})$ *is* a good approximation to $B(20, 0.5).$ \n\n![](images/9e/9efc7df7c423ed2d8d6cc70ef820de3cb235f644567a3fec0d0a0e501042e305.png)\n\nThe fact that we can approximate binomial random variables as normal when $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5$ is a consequence of the so-called **central limit theorem** (or CLT). We'll discuss this in more detail at the end of the lesson." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116536 #edb/ref 1065 4970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116536 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4d16ee6a-2c83-55f2-a37f-2b5264587984" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116536 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116542 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a random variable $X \\sim B(50,0.6).$ Let's estimate the value of $P(X=33)$ using the normal approximation.\n\nGiven a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np > 5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu = np$ and variance $\\sigma^2 = np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY \\sim N(np, np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=50$ and $p = 0.6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =30 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50\\cdot (1-0.6) \\\\  & =20 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu = 50\\cdot0.6 = 30\n$$\n\nand variance \n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = 50\\cdot0.6\\cdot(1-0.6)=12.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by \n\n$$\nY \\sim N(30,12).\n$$\n\nNow, since $X$ is discrete and $Y$ is continuous, we need to apply a continuity correction. The required continuity correction in this case is\n\n$$\nP(X = 33) \\approx P(32.5 \\leq Y \\leq 33.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(32.5\\le Y\\le 33.5) & =P(\\frac{32.5-30}{\\sqrt{12}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{33.5-30}{\\sqrt{12}}) \\\\  & ≈P(0.72\\le Z\\le 1.01) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.01)-P(Z<0.72) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.01)-P(Z\\le 0.72) \\\\  & =Φ(1.01)-Φ(0.72).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a table of values for the CDF of the standard normal distribution, we find that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(1.01) = 0.8438, \\qquad \\Phi(0.72) = 0.7642.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(32.5\\le Y\\le 33.5) & =Φ(1.01)-Φ(0.72) \\\\  & =0.8438-0.7642 \\\\  & =0.0796.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X = 33) \\approx 0.0796.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116542 #edb/ref 1065 5618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116542 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "22b0d565-b5ca-5c37-9d8a-f8a2c8f83fce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116542 #edb/ref 1067 "Using the Normal Approximation to the Binomial Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116543 #edb/ref 1064 "In the last example, we approximated the binomial random variable\n\n$$\nX \\sim B(50,0.6)\n$$\n\nusing a normal random variable\n\n$$\nY \\sim N(30,12),\n$$\n\nand we arrived at the approximation\n\n$$\nP(X=33) \\approx P(32.5 \\leq Y \\leq 33.5)  \\approx 0.0796.\n$$\n\nIf you were to use a software package to evaluate this approximation, you might get an answer like\n\n$$\nP(X = 33) \\approx P(32.5 \\leq Y \\leq 33.5)  \\approx 0.0791.\n$$\n\nNotice that there is a difference of around $0.0005$ between the two answers.\n\nThere is a reason for this discrepancy. When we calculated that $P(X = 33) \\approx P(32.5 \\leq Y \\leq 33.5)  \\approx 0.0796,$ we rounded our $z$-values two decimal places and then used a lookup table to find $\\Phi(z)$ for those rounded values. As a result, we introduced some small rounding errors. \n\nTypically, the rounding errors are small, so we will continue to work with a table of values. However, it's worth bearing this in mind if you're using a software package or calculator to compute $\\Phi(z).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116543 #edb/ref 1065 5619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116543 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d460cc56-a34f-5495-874f-19d80c5ad3b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116543 #edb/ref 1067 "A Note on Rounding Errors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116546 #edb/ref 1064 "According to the so-called **central limit theorem**, if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots X_n$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) random variables, then\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\approx N(n\\mu, n\\sigma^2),\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is \"sufficiently large,\" and\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\mu, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\sigma^2.\n$$\n\nNow, if $X\\sim B(n,p),$ then $X$ can be thought of as a sum of $n$ I.I.D Bernoulli random variables \n\n$$\nX_i\\sim\\textrm{Bernoulli}(p),\n$$\n\nwhere the PMF of each $X_i$ is given by\n\n![](images/13/134fcb82e69ef8d5fc1e63353f3b28c036bda08176918bcd166ebc52826854c6.svg)\n\nIt can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\mu = p, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\sigma^2 = p(1-p).\n$$\n\nTherefore, by substituting our values of $\\mu$ and $\\sigma^2$ into our normal approximation for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\nX \\approx N\\left(np, np(1-p)\\right).\n$$\n\nThe \"sufficiently large\" condition on $n$ is taken care of by stipulating that $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- When $p\\approx 0.5,$ then $n\\approx 10$ is sufficiently large for the normal approximation to be applied.\n\n- However, if $p$ is closer to zero or one, then the distribution of $X$ is not symmetric, and consequently, we need $n$ to be much larger for the central limit theorem to be valid." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116546 #edb/ref 1065 6514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116546 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9a338343-d2c2-54a6-98b5-5033bebeb652" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116546 #edb/ref 1067 "The Normal Approximation as a Consequence of the Central Limit Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506672 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186507541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506672 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186507556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506672 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186507559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506672 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186507582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506672 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186507585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506672 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506672 #edb/ref 1045 "Normal Approximations of Binomial Distributions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506672 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186507541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506672 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506672 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506672 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "a837cf52-5fb1-5898-afea-41992db6bf3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506684 #edb/ref 1080 "0.646" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506684 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506684 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04178a47-fcec-5ede-9b71-98ed6cbe2fcc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506728 #edb/ref 1080 "0.050" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506728 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506728 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1b87403b-2d5c-5958-adc4-7d80cda317ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506733 #edb/ref 1080 "0.450" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506733 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506733 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1cc81458-aad3-538d-a9ec-87e68bf313f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506798 #edb/ref 1080 "0.659" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506798 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506798 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "326999d2-ac2e-53ac-ac17-d513127bd271" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506815 #edb/ref 1080 "0.145" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506815 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506815 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3ad8adae-1cb9-5c58-a3e8-10184552fc05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506839 #edb/